@n/

@* Introduction.

\ID{$Id$}

We define the various classes here and the methods to access the
components.  Each class has a two-letter abbreviation which is used to
prefix the accessor functions.  Each class has 5 standard methods:

|CL_args(inst)| is how to pass a instance of a class to a subroutine.
|CL_dummy(inst)| is the dummy argument for a subroutine.
|CL_common| will declare common variable for this class.
|CL_decl(inst)| is how to declare a member of a class in a subroutine.
|CL_copy(inst1,inst2)| is how to copy one instance of a class to
another instance of the same class.
|CL_check(inst)| will check the consistency of the instance.

The instance above is designated in one of two ways.

For some classes, the information is held in global common blocks.  In
this case, the instance is denoted by an integer variable which is a
pointer into the common block arrays.  Examples of these classes are
elements, species, and reactions.

For the other classes, the instance is tagged with an arbitrary string.
The various components are obtained by constructing variable names from
that string.

In both cases, the instance can be an array reference and an array of
instances can be constructed in a natural manner; e.g.,
|CL_args(inst[3])| or |CL_decl(inst[0:5])|.

These combine in a natural way.  Thus if we are dealing with flight |f|,
then |fl_current(f)| is the current particle being tracked.  Its species
is |pt_sp(fl_current(f))|.  The first component of this species is
|sp_el(pt_sp(fl_current(f)),1)|, and so on.

@m FILE 'classes.web'

@ Element class.  This defines elements.  Prefix is |el|.  The
identifier |element| is an integer variable.

|el_name(element)| returns the long name (length |el_name_len|).
|el_sy(element)| returns the symbolic name (length |el_sy_len|).
|el_z(element)| returns the charge number.
|el_m(element)| returns the mass number (in amu).

@ Species class.  Prefix is |sp|.  The identifier |species| is an
integer variable.

|sp_name(species)| returns the long name (length |sp_name_len|).
|sp_sy(species)| returns the symbolic name (length |sp_sy_len|).
|sp_z(species)| returns the charge number.
|sp_m(species)| returns the mass number (in amu).
|sp_ncomp(species)| returns the number of components in a species
(maximum size |sp_ncomp_max|).
|sp_el(species,i)| returns the element for the $i$th component.
|sp_count(species,i)| returns the count of the $i$th component.

@ Particle class.  Prefix is |pt|.  The identifier |particle| is a
string tag.

|pt_sp(particle)| is the species of the particle.
|pt_t(particle)| is the time for this particle.
|pt_w(particle)| is the statistical weight of the particle.
|pt_v(particle)| is the velocity of the particle.
|pt_loc(particle)| is the location of the particle (see below).
|pt_type(particle)| is the type of tracking to be done for the particle.

@ Routines involving particles.

@ Location class.  Prefix is |lc|.  The identifier |location| is a
string tag.

|lc_x(location)| is the position.
|lc_cell(location)| is the cell number.
|lc_zone(location)| is the zone number.
|lc_face(location)| is the face of the cell if at a face (else 0).
|lc_cell_next(location)| is the next cell number.
|lc_zone_next(location)| is the next zone number.

@ Routines using locations.

|lc_set(x)| sets other fields given the position.

@ Flight class. Prefix is |fl|.  The identifier |flight| is a string tag.

|fl_number(flight)| is the flight number.
|fl_origin(flight)| is the particle initiating the flight.
|fl_rand(flight)| is the random number information for the flight.
|fl_stack(flight)| is the stack of particles being processed.
|fl_pointer(flight)| is the pointer to the current particle (maximum size
|fl_stack_max|).
|fl_current(flight)| is the current particle (same as
|fl_stack(flight)[fl_pointer(flight)]|).

@ Reaction class.  Prefix is |rc|.  The identifier |reaction| is an
integer variable.

|rc_name(reaction)| is the name of the reaction (length |rc_name_len|).
|rc_sy(reaction)| is the symbolic name of the reaction (length |rc_sy_len|).
|rc_reagant_num(reaction)| is the number of reagants in the reaction
(maximum value |rc_reagant_max|).
|rc_reagant(reaction,i)| is the $i$th species reagant in the reaction.
|rc_product_num(reaction)| is the number of products (maximum value
|rc_product_max|).
|rc_product(reaction,i)| is the species of the $i$th product.

@ Background class.  Prefix is |bk|.  The identifier |background| is an
integer variable.

|bk_sp(background)| is the species of the background.
|bk_n(background,i)| is the density in the $i$th zone.
|bk_v(background,i)| is the velocity in the $i$th zone.
|bk_temp(background,i)| is the temperature in the $i$th zone.

@ Zone class.  Prefix is |zn|.  The identifier |zone| is an integer variable.

|zn_type(zone)| is one of |zn_vacuum|, |zn_plasma|, |zn_solid|
indicating the type of zone.
|zn_index_i(zone,l)| for $l=1,$|zn_index_max| gives the fluid code
indices of the zone.
|zn_pointer(zone)| is a pointer into arrays specific for different types
of zones: plasma, solid, etc.

@ Random class.  Prefix is |rn|.  The identifier |random| is a string.

|rn_index(random)| index to next random number.
|rn_max(random)| maximum index computed.
|rn_count(random)| count of random numbers computed.
|rn_array(random)| array of random numbers.
|rn_gauss_index(random)| index to next gaussion random number.
|rn_gauss_array(random)| array of gaussian random numbers.
|rn_iso_index(random)| index to next isotropically distributed vectors.
|rn_iso_array(random)| array of isotropically distributibuted vectors.
|rn_cos_index(random)| index to next cosine distributed vectors.
|rn_cos_array(random)| array of cosine distributibuted vectors.

@ Routines using the random numbers.

|rn_init(seed,random)| initialize random numbers from 2-element integer array
|seed|.

|rn_next(x,random)| next random number into $x$ (inline available).

|rn_fast_next(x,random)| fast version of this (inline available).

|rn_check(n,random)| check |n| numbers available for |rn_fast_next|.

|rn_gauss_next(x,random)| next gaussian number in $x$ (inline availble).

|rn_iso_next(v,random)| next isotropic vector number into $\vect v$
(inline availble).

|rn_cos_next(v,random)| next cosine vector number into $\vect v$
(inline availble).

|call random_seed(seed)| initialize seed from current time, etc.

|cell next_seed(n,seed,newseed)| advance |seed| forward $n$ steps.

@i classes.hweb
